"""Regenerate one rev-3 quantity layer's overviews, catalog and tile indexes.

Design section 9: a regenerate is a general store process, driven by
fingerprints rather than mtimes, rebuilding only what changed. This is that
process for ``depths/``, as a Snakemake workflow over the per-parent work unit
`build_depth_overview_parent` provides.

Run it as::

    snakemake -s marine_world_store/snakemake/Snakefile -j8 \\
        --config layer_dir=<a quantity layer> fine_level=<its finest level> \\
                 min_level=8

``layer_dir`` is REQUIRED and has no default. The store root is resolved by
``marine_world_store.store_root`` wherever a tool needs it; nothing here writes
a path down, which is what the repo's literal-path guard test enforces.

Shape of the DAG, and why it is shaped that way:

1. The fingerprint pre-step runs when this file is LOADED, before Snakemake
   plans anything. Snakemake decides from mtimes; section 9 decides from
   fingerprints. The pre-step decides from each tile's CONTENT (its ``.fp``
   sidecar) and then sets the mtime to say so: a tile rewritten byte for byte
   goes back to its recorded mtime, and a changed native tile is advanced to
   now -- including one copied in with an OLD mtime (``copy2``, ``rsync -t``,
   a restore), which by mtime alone would look older than its products and be
   missed. A DERIVED tile whose content is not the recorded content (a
   restore, a partial copy, bit rot) is removed with its record, and rebuilt
   as a missing product: advanced, it would look newer than its children and
   never be rebuilt. A tile a rule builds is recorded as built (``mws_refresh_fingerprints
   --record``), so it stays newer than the children it absorbed even when it
   came out byte for byte the same. As a rule inside the DAG the pre-step could
   not work: Snakemake compares
   mtimes while PLANNING, before any rule runs, so a reconciliation scheduled
   as a job always comes too late -- and a rule with no inputs and a stamp
   output ran once and never again. An invocation that does not execute the
   workflow -- a dry run, ``--summary``, ``--list-*-changes``, ``--dag``,
   ``--lint``, ``--unlock`` and the like, as Snakemake itself parsed them --
   skips it, and the listing reset, and every other write this file makes
   when it loads except ``.regenerate/`` itself (Snakemake's ``workdir:``
   creates it) and its lock.

2. Every rule's inputs and outputs are the real files. A parent tile's rule
   takes the child TILES it folds as inputs and declares the tile and its
   record as outputs, so a changed child reruns exactly the parents above it
   and nothing else; a vanished or added child changes the parent's recorded
   input set, which reruns it too (Snakemake's default ``input`` rerun
   trigger). The first version declared only ``.done`` stamps, which satisfied
   everything after the first run: a rewritten tile produced "Nothing to be
   done".

3. Each level is a ``checkpoint``. The parents that exist at level N cannot be
   known until level N+1 has been built, because a derived tile at N+1 is
   itself a contributor. The checkpoint first PRUNES level N -- removing
   derived tiles a native tile now covers or whose children are gone, which no
   per-parent job would ever be scheduled to remove -- then lists level N's
   parents WITH their children (`build_depth_overview_parent --prune` and
   `--list-parents`), so the GGGS parent/child mapping is never re-implemented
   here. Derived levels coarser than ``min_level`` are removed when this file
   loads (``--remove-level``): a run configured differently from the last
   would otherwise leave whole levels that nothing rebuilds, still published
   by the manifest and the Items. A derived tile at or finer than
   ``fine_level``, or a native tile finer than it, is refused by name before
   anything is removed.

4. ``assemble_coverage`` is a single serialised step AFTER every parent. The
   per-parent writer deliberately leaves a per-tile record instead of touching
   a shared ``coverage.json``, because parallel writers would race over that one
   file; this turns the records into the manifest uma-ADR-0013 D3 expects. Its
   output is a stamp (``coverage.done``) deleted when this file loads, so it
   runs every run; ``coverage.json`` is not a declared output, because
   Snakemake would touch it after the job even when its bytes did not change.

5. ``catalog`` builds the overview tiles' Items and rewrites only changed ones
   (section 9's replica rule) -- for THIS layer (``--layer-dir``), not for
   whatever tree the environment's store root names.

6. The tile indexes are derived from the Items and never synced (design
   section 7): regenerated locally, one per band schema. See ``rules/gti.smk``
   for the GDAL version they need to be read as rasters.

Every level's listing is re-derived on every run, and that is what makes a
MISSING product rebuild. The listings are deleted when this file loads and are
targets of ``rule all`` (with ``products.done``, a rule that asks for every
derived tile and its record), so every level's checkpoint runs; its listing
then names every parent the level should hold, and a derived tile or per-tile
record deleted since the last run is a missing output of a scheduled job,
which Snakemake rebuilds. Deleting the listings was not enough on its own: a
checkpoint's output is only produced when something ASKS for it, and when
every product downstream exists Snakemake asks for nothing -- so a deleted
tile got "Nothing to be done" while the manifest and its Item kept
advertising it. (A dry run keeps the listings, and so may plan from a stale
one.) Listing is a directory scan, cheap next to a fold, and the parents'
jobs do not depend on the listing FILE, so a re-derived listing rebuilds no
tile by itself. It does make the bookkeeping steps -- manifest, catalog,
indexes -- run on every run; the manifest and the Items/Collection are
rewritten only when their content changed, so an unchanged layer's published
files keep their bytes and mtimes (section 9's replica rule).

Two runs over one layer are serialised by a lock taken when this file loads
(``.regenerate/regenerate.lock``), before the pre-step or the listing reset
touch anything, whatever directory each run was started from -- the first
version had nothing but Snakemake's per-working-directory ``.snakemake/``
lock, so two runs from two directories interleaved. ``workdir:`` also puts
Snakemake's metadata (the input sets its rerun triggers compare) in the
layer's ``.regenerate/``, so it is the same record whoever runs it. (The C++
writers additionally hold ``<layer>/overviews.lock`` against a concurrent
batch build.)

Snakemake removes a job's existing outputs before running it, so an overview
tile being rebuilt is briefly absent, and a failed rebuild leaves it absent
until the next run. Overviews are derived and regenerable; a reader of them
falls back to the level below.
"""

import fcntl
import os
from pathlib import Path
import re
import shlex
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys

configfile_layer = config.get("layer_dir")
if not configfile_layer:
    raise WorkflowError(
        "layer_dir is required: --config layer_dir=<quantity layer>. There is "
        "no default, because the store root is resolved by "
        "marine_world_store.store_root and a path written down here would be "
        "the hard-coded path the guard test forbids."
    )

LAYER_DIR = Path(str(configfile_layer)).expanduser().resolve()
if not LAYER_DIR.is_dir():
    raise WorkflowError(f"layer_dir {LAYER_DIR} is not a directory")
OVERVIEWS = LAYER_DIR / "overviews"

#: Coarsest level to build (0 = apex). The finest level is DISCOVERED from the
#: layer; asserting it would be meaningless for a mixed-level layer.
MIN_LEVEL = int(config.get("min_level", 0))

#: The layer's finest native level, needed to know where the ladder starts.
#: Supplied by the caller: reading it here would mean a scan at DAG-build time,
#: and `build_depth_overviews --dry-run` already reports it for an operator who
#: does not know it.
FINE_LEVEL = config.get("fine_level")

#: ``<level>_<row>_<col>.tif`` exactly -- the canonical tile name.
_TILE_NAME = re.compile(r"(0|[1-9]\d*)_(0|[1-9]\d*)_(0|[1-9]\d*)\.tif")


def _tile_level(path):
    """The level of a canonically named tile, else ``None``."""
    match = _TILE_NAME.fullmatch(path.name)
    return int(match.group(1)) if match else None


if FINE_LEVEL is not None:
    try:
        FINE_LEVEL = int(FINE_LEVEL)
    except ValueError:
        raise WorkflowError(f"fine_level={FINE_LEVEL!r} is not a level")
    # Checked before anything is written or removed. A fine_level coarser than
    # the natives is a config slip, not a request: the pyramid would be built
    # from the wrong level, and the removal of derived levels this run does
    # not build would empty the published catalog -- exit 0.
    _finer = sorted(
        path.name for path in LAYER_DIR.glob("*_*_*.tif")
        if (_tile_level(path) or 0) > FINE_LEVEL)
    if _finer:
        _named = ", ".join(_finer[:3]) + (", ..." if len(_finer) > 3 else "")
        raise WorkflowError(
            f"fine_level={FINE_LEVEL}, but {LAYER_DIR} holds native tiles at "
            f"a finer level ({_named}): refusing before anything is removed. "
            "Pass the layer's finest native level; "
            "`build_depth_overviews --dry-run <layer>` reports it.")
    # A derived tile at or finer than fine_level has no native tile below it
    # (checked above), so it describes nothing a fold made -- a pyramid built
    # under another fine_level, or a stray file. Named, not guessed at: the
    # levels removed automatically are only the coarse ones min_level drops.
    _derived_finer = sorted(
        path.name for path in OVERVIEWS.glob("*_*_*.tif")
        if (_tile_level(path) if _tile_level(path) is not None else -1)
        >= FINE_LEVEL)
    if _derived_finer:
        _named = (", ".join(_derived_finer[:3]) +
                  (", ..." if len(_derived_finer) > 3 else ""))
        raise WorkflowError(
            f"{OVERVIEWS} holds derived tiles at or finer than "
            f"fine_level={FINE_LEVEL} ({_named}), which no native tile lies "
            "below: refusing before anything is removed. Remove them (with "
            "their .json records and .fp sidecars), or pass the fine_level "
            "they were built under.")

#: Where the run's own bookkeeping goes -- and Snakemake's, via ``workdir:``.
#: Inside the layer, so a layer carries its regenerate state with it, two
#: layers never share a DAG's scratch, and two runs over ONE layer share one
#: lock whatever directory each was started from.
WORK = LAYER_DIR / ".regenerate"
WORK.mkdir(exist_ok=True)

#: Held for the life of this Snakemake process; the kernel releases it on exit.
_RUN_LOCK = open(WORK / "regenerate.lock", "a")
try:
    fcntl.flock(_RUN_LOCK, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
except BlockingIOError:
    raise WorkflowError(
        f"another regenerate is running over {LAYER_DIR} "
        f"({WORK / 'regenerate.lock'} is held); retry when it finishes")

#: The arguments of Snakemake 7's ``snakemake()`` that mean "do not execute
#: the workflow": a dry run, a query, a report, a cleanup. Any one of them set
#: makes this invocation read-only as far as this file is concerned.
_NON_EXECUTING = (
    "dryrun", "touch", "summary", "detailed_summary", "list_version_changes",
    "list_code_changes", "list_input_changes", "list_params_changes",
    "list_untracked", "list_resources", "listrules", "list_target_rules",
    "printdag", "printrulegraph", "printfilegraph", "printd3dag", "lint",
    "unlock", "cleanup_metadata", "conda_cleanup_envs", "cleanup_shadow",
    "cleanup_containers", "archive", "delete_all_output",
    "delete_temp_output", "print_compilation", "report", "containerize",
    "generate_unit_tests", "list_conda_envs", "conda_create_envs_only",
    "export_cwl",
)


def _executing():
    """
    Whether this invocation will execute the workflow, as Snakemake decided.

    Read from the arguments Snakemake's own ``snakemake()`` call is running
    with -- after its parser, its argument abbreviations and any profile have
    been applied -- rather than guessed from ``sys.argv``: a regex over argv
    missed ``--dry-ru`` and every profile, read ``-sSnakefile`` as a dry run,
    and knew nothing of ``--summary`` and the other query modes. A job
    Snakemake re-invokes this file for (``mode`` other than the default: a
    cluster or subprocess job) is not the run either.
    """
    frame = sys._getframe()
    while frame is not None:
        if (frame.f_code.co_name == "snakemake" and
                frame.f_globals.get("__name__") == "snakemake"):
            invocation = frame.f_locals
            if str(invocation.get("mode", 0)) not in ("0", "Mode.default"):
                return False
            return not any(invocation.get(name) for name in _NON_EXECUTING)
        frame = frame.f_back
    raise WorkflowError(
        "cannot read this Snakemake's invocation, so cannot tell a real run "
        "from a dry run or a query; the regenerate is written for Snakemake "
        "7's snakemake() entry point (the declared dependency)")


EXECUTING = _executing()


# Resolved BEFORE ``workdir:``, which changes directory: a relative
# ``--config <tool>_tool=`` is relative to where snakemake was started, as
# ``layer_dir`` is.
def _tool(name, package):
    """
    Locate one of the tools the rules run.

    ``--config <name>_tool=<path>`` wins; then ``PATH``; then the ROS install
    space, where colcon puts both packages' executables under
    ``<prefix>/lib/<package>/`` -- NOT on ``PATH``, so a rule that ran the bare
    name worked only for whoever happened to have that directory there.
    """
    override = config.get(f"{name}_tool")
    if override:
        path = Path(str(override)).expanduser().resolve()
        if not (path.is_file() and os.access(path, os.X_OK)):
            raise WorkflowError(f"{name}_tool={override}: not an executable")
        return str(path)
    found = shutil.which(name)
    if found:
        # Absolute NOW: a relative PATH entry is relative to where snakemake
        # was started, and ``workdir:`` is about to change directory.
        return os.path.abspath(found)
    for prefix in os.environ.get("AMENT_PREFIX_PATH", "").split(os.pathsep):
        candidate = Path(prefix) / "lib" / package / name if prefix else None
        if candidate and candidate.is_file() and os.access(candidate, os.X_OK):
            return str(candidate)
    raise WorkflowError(
        f"cannot find {name} (package {package}): source the workspace's "
        f"install/setup.bash, put it on PATH, or pass --config "
        f"{name}_tool=<path>"
    )


def _shell_literal(value):
    """A value quoted for the shell and escaped for Snakemake's formatting."""
    return shlex.quote(str(value)).replace("{", "{{").replace("}", "}}")


OVERVIEW_TOOL = _tool("build_depth_overview_parent", "marine_bathymetry_store")
REFRESH_TOOL = _tool("mws_refresh_fingerprints", "marine_world_store")
ASSEMBLE_TOOL = _tool("mws_assemble_coverage", "marine_world_store")
CATALOG_TOOL = _tool("mws_regenerate_catalog", "marine_world_store")
LIST_TILES_TOOL = _tool("mws_list_tiles", "marine_world_store")

workdir: str(WORK)


wildcard_constraints:
    level=r"\d+",
    row=r"\d+",
    col=r"\d+",


if not EXECUTING:
    print("not executing (a dry run or a query): the fingerprint pre-step is "
          "skipped (it writes .fp files, moves mtimes and removes untrusted "
          "derived tiles), so a tile rewritten byte for byte may be listed "
          "as a change it is not", file=sys.stderr)
else:
    # Derived levels coarser than min_level are no longer built: remove them
    # now, before the pre-step reads them and before anything is planned, so
    # nothing publishes them. (In the finest level's listing job, as it first
    # was, this never ran when min_level >= fine_level: no listing job.)
    if FINE_LEVEL is not None:
        _dropped = sorted({
            _tile_level(path) for path in OVERVIEWS.glob("*_*_*.tif")
            if _tile_level(path) is not None
            and _tile_level(path) < MIN_LEVEL})
        for _level in _dropped:
            subprocess.run(
                [OVERVIEW_TOOL, "--remove-level", str(LAYER_DIR), str(_level)],
                check=True)
    if str(config.get("refresh_fingerprints", True)).lower() not in (
            "false", "0", "no"):
        subprocess.run([REFRESH_TOOL, str(LAYER_DIR)], check=True)
    # Re-derive every level's listing (see the module docstring).
    for stale_listing in WORK.glob("parents_*.tsv"):
        stale_listing.unlink()
    for stamp in ("products.done", "coverage.done"):
        (WORK / stamp).unlink(missing_ok=True)


def native_tiles(level):
    """The layer's NATIVE tiles at ``level`` (the compile; no rule makes them)."""
    return sorted(
        path for path in LAYER_DIR.glob(f"{int(level)}_*_*.tif")
        if _TILE_NAME.fullmatch(path.name))


include: "rules/overviews.smk"
include: "rules/catalog.smk"
include: "rules/gti.smk"


rule all:
    input:
        # The listings are targets so every level's checkpoint runs every run
        # (see the module docstring): that is what notices a missing product.
        expand(str(WORK / "parents_{level}.tsv"), level=_levels()),
        WORK / "products.done",
        WORK / "catalog.done",
        WORK / "gti_native.done",
        WORK / "gti_overview.done",
